2025-05-01 02:55:41 - Thread-1072 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage  

• SALES_ORDERS."OrderID": primary key for each order; will be used to identify orders that never became invoices.  
• SALES_ORDERS."CustomerID": lets us trace the order back to a customer.  
• SALES_INVOICES."OrderID": appears only when an order has been invoiced; absence means the order is “lost.”  
• SALES_ORDERLINES."OrderID": joins order lines to their header so we can price each lost order.  
• SALES_ORDERLINES."Quantity": number of units on a line – required for value calculation.  
• SALES_ORDERLINES."UnitPrice": price per unit – multiply with Quantity for a line’s value.  
• SALES_CUSTOMERS."CustomerID": connects an order to the customer master table.  
• SALES_CUSTOMERS."CustomerCategoryID": maps the customer to its category.  
• SALES_CUSTOMERCATEGORIES."CustomerCategoryID": category key.  
• SALES_CUSTOMERCATEGORIES."CustomerCategoryName": human‑readable name, used in the final presentation.  

Typical conditions we will need  
• SALES_ORDERS."OrderID" NOT IN (SELECT DISTINCT "OrderID" FROM SALES_INVOICES …) – to isolate lost orders.  
• Inner joins on identical keys ("OrderID", "CustomerID", "CustomerCategoryID").  
• GROUP BY / MAX / AVG to get “maximum lost order value” per category and then the overall average.  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries that move step‑by‑step from simple inspection to the final answer. All column names are wrapped in double quotes, every SELECT that can return many rows is capped with LIMIT 20 so you can preview the data before running full‑scale analyses.

```sql
-- 1) Peek at some customer categories
--Description: Show a sample of customer categories that will appear in later joins.
SELECT
    "CustomerCategoryID",
    "CustomerCategoryName"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_CUSTOMERCATEGORIES
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Inspect orders and their customers
--Description: Look at a few orders together with their customers and categories.
SELECT
    o."OrderID",
    o."CustomerID",
    c."CustomerCategoryID"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS o
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_CUSTOMERS c
      ON o."CustomerID" = c."CustomerID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) View orders that already have invoices
--Description: Confirm the link between the two tables; rows returned here are NOT lost.
SELECT DISTINCT
    i."OrderID"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_INVOICES i
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS  o
      ON i."OrderID" = o."OrderID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) List orders that never became invoices (candidate lost orders)
--Description: First glimpse of the “lost orders” population.
SELECT
    o."OrderID",
    o."CustomerID"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS o
LEFT JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_INVOICES i
       ON o."OrderID" = i."OrderID"
WHERE i."OrderID" IS NULL          -- not invoiced
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Attach order lines to those lost orders
--Description: See the line‑level detail involved in lost orders.
SELECT
    ol."OrderLineID",
    ol."OrderID",
    ol."Quantity",
    ol."UnitPrice"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERLINES ol
WHERE ol."OrderID" IN (
      SELECT o."OrderID"
      FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS o
      LEFT JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_INVOICES i
             ON o."OrderID" = i."OrderID"
      WHERE i."OrderID" IS NULL )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Compute a provisional value per lost order line
--Description: Create a “LineValue” column (= Quantity * UnitPrice) for lost orders.
SELECT
    ol."OrderID",
    ol."Quantity",
    ol."UnitPrice",
    (ol."Quantity" * ol."UnitPrice")   AS "LineValue"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERLINES ol
WHERE ol."OrderID" IN (
      SELECT o."OrderID"
      FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS o
      LEFT JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_INVOICES i
             ON o."OrderID" = i."OrderID"
      WHERE i."OrderID" IS NULL )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Aggregate lost order value per order
--Description: Sum the line values so we know the dollar amount of each lost order.
SELECT
    ol."OrderID",
    SUM(ol."Quantity" * ol."UnitPrice") AS "LostOrderValue"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERLINES ol
WHERE ol."OrderID" IN (
      SELECT o."OrderID"
      FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS o
      LEFT JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_INVOICES i
             ON o."OrderID" = i."OrderID"
      WHERE i."OrderID" IS NULL )
GROUP BY ol."OrderID"
ORDER BY "LostOrderValue" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Map each lost order to its customer category and compute value
--Description: Prepares data for the MAX() per category step.
SELECT
    c."CustomerCategoryID",
    SUM(ol."Quantity" * ol."UnitPrice") AS "LostOrderValue"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERLINES ol
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS o
        ON ol."OrderID" = o."OrderID"
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_CUSTOMERS c
        ON o."CustomerID" = c."CustomerID"
LEFT JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_INVOICES i
        ON o."OrderID" = i."OrderID"
WHERE i."OrderID" IS NULL
GROUP BY c."CustomerCategoryID", ol."OrderID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Find the maximum lost order value inside each customer category
--Description: Produces one row per category containing its worst lost order.
SELECT
    ccat."CustomerCategoryID",
    ccat."CustomerCategoryName",
    MAX(lo."LostOrderValue") AS "MaxLostOrderValue"
FROM (
      SELECT
          c."CustomerCategoryID",
          SUM(ol."Quantity" * ol."UnitPrice") AS "LostOrderValue"
      FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERLINES ol
      JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS o
              ON ol."OrderID" = o."OrderID"
      JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_CUSTOMERS c
              ON o."CustomerID" = c."CustomerID"
      LEFT JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_INVOICES i
              ON o."OrderID" = i."OrderID"
      WHERE i."OrderID" IS NULL
      GROUP BY c."CustomerCategoryID", ol."OrderID"
) lo
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_CUSTOMERCATEGORIES ccat
     ON lo."CustomerCategoryID" = ccat."CustomerCategoryID"
GROUP BY ccat."CustomerCategoryID", ccat."CustomerCategoryName"
ORDER BY "MaxLostOrderValue" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Determine the category whose MaxLostOrderValue is closest to the overall average
--Description: Final answer query; computes the average of category maxima and finds the nearest one.
WITH category_max AS (
    SELECT
        c."CustomerCategoryID",
        MAX(SUM(ol."Quantity" * ol."UnitPrice")) AS "MaxLostOrderValue"
    FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERLINES ol
    JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_ORDERS o
            ON ol."OrderID" = o."OrderID"
    JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_CUSTOMERS cu
            ON o."CustomerID" = cu."CustomerID"
    LEFT JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_INVOICES i
            ON o."OrderID" = i."OrderID"
    WHERE i."OrderID" IS NULL
    GROUP BY cu."CustomerCategoryID", ol."OrderID"     -- first level (per order)
    GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((cu."CustomerCategoryID"))
), avg_calc AS (
    SELECT AVG("MaxLostOrderValue") AS "AvgOfMax" FROM category_max
)
SELECT
    cm."CustomerCategoryID",
    sc."CustomerCategoryName",
    cm."MaxLostOrderValue",
    ac."AvgOfMax",
    ABS(cm."MaxLostOrderValue" - ac."AvgOfMax") AS "DistanceToAvg"
FROM category_max cm
CROSS JOIN avg_calc ac
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.SALES_CUSTOMERCATEGORIES sc
     ON cm."CustomerCategoryID" = sc."CustomerCategoryID"
ORDER BY "DistanceToAvg"
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries let you progressively explore the data, verify assumptions, and ultimately derive which customer category’s maximum lost order value sits closest to the overall average of such maxima.
